     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\">On Tue, Apr 11, 2017 at 2:26 PM, balal izanloo <span dir=\"ltr\">&lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">b.ez...@gmail.com</a>&gt;</span> wrote:<br><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div style=\"font-size:large;color:rgb(0,0,255)\"><div>Greeting </div><div><br></div><div style=\"color:rgb(34,34,34);font-size:12.8px\"><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><font color=\"#0000ff\"><span style=\"font-size:18pt\">I am comparing 3PL with 4PL in my analysis. your package&#39;s mirt can handle estimation of parameters in 1 to 4 models. i need to your help by answering to my </span><span style=\"font-size:24px\">questions</span><span style=\"font-size:18pt\">: </span></font></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><font color=\"#0000ff\"><span style=\"font-size:18pt\"><br></span></font></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><font color=\"#0000ff\"><span style=\"font-size:18pt\">1) i need to </span><span style=\"font-size:24px\">compare</span><span style=\"font-size:18pt\"> models based on </span><span style=\"font-size:24px\">their</span><span style=\"font-size:18pt\"> fit, for example by -2logliklihood, is it </span><span style=\"font-size:24px\">possible by mirt&#39;s functions</span><span style=\"font-size:18pt\">?</span></font></p></div></div></div></div></blockquote><div><br></div><div>Yes. Though for bounded parameter comparisons you&#39;ll likely need to use the bounded = TRUE input to anova().</div><div> </div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div style=\"font-size:large;color:rgb(0,0,255)\"><div style=\"color:rgb(34,34,34);font-size:12.8px\"><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><font color=\"#0000ff\"><span style=\"font-size:18pt\"><u></u></span></font></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><font color=\"#0000ff\"><span style=\"font-size:18pt\">2) can i reach to items and test information values and plots, </span><span style=\"font-size:24px\">especially</span><span style=\"font-size:18pt\"> for 4PL?</span></font></p></div></div></div></div></blockquote><div><br></div><div>Yes. </div><div><br></div><div> </div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div style=\"font-size:large;color:rgb(0,0,255)\"><div style=\"color:rgb(34,34,34);font-size:12.8px\"><div style=\"font-size:12.8px\"><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:18pt;color:blue\"><u></u> <u></u></span></p></div><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><font color=\"#0000ff\"><span style=\"font-size:18pt\">3) i need to fix the lower asymptotic parameter to zero for 4 parameters model, is it </span><span style=\"font-size:24px\">possible</span><span style=\"font-size:18pt\">?</span></font></p></div></div></div></div></blockquote><div><br></div><div>Yes. See also the itemtype = &#39;3PLu&#39; input. </div><div><br></div><div>Also, please use the mirt-package forum next time instead if you have general questions. Cheers.</div><div><br></div><div>Phil</div><div> </div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div style=\"font-size:large;color:rgb(0,0,255)\"><div style=\"color:rgb(34,34,34);font-size:12.8px\"><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"font-size:12.8px\"><span style=\"color:blue;font-size:18pt\"><br></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"font-size:12.8px\"><span style=\"color:blue;font-size:18pt\">Regard</span></p></div></div></div></div>\r\n</blockquote></div><br></div></div>\r\n"
